        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-12-31T05:42:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Will things continue to work if an application passes in a NetworkRequest created by:\n\n    NetworkRequest r \u003d new NetworkRequest.Builder().setNetworkSpecifier(\"1\").build();\n\n? AFAIK that code will result in the NetworkRequest having a StringNetworkSpecifier of \"1\", and that request will never match this network.\n\n\nI don\u0027t currently see another way to do this except:\n\n1. Add a hack to StringNetworkSpecifier to specifically match TelephonyNetworkSpecifier objects.\n2. Change the semantics of StringNetworkSpecifier.equals() so that it does something like:\n    return equals(other) ||\n            (other instanceof NetworkSpecifier \u0026\u0026 specifier.equals(other.toString()));\n3. (Probably too hacky) Make TelephonyNetworkSpecifier subclass StringNetworkSpecifier.\n\nPersonally I\u0027d prefer #2 because it is the smallest application-visible change and it maintains the current semantics of NetworkSpecifiers, which only match if the class is the same.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1319,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1320,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "cef2fccf376f97d07121e7aefddac23c8f116bfb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6fca0645_1258bef7",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/dataconnection/DataConnection.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 1320,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-02T19:41:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Couple questions here:\n1) Is it actually valid for a caller to expect that a specifier .equals() for a network request and a network callback? I\u0027d assert that a specifier for A that is satisfied by another app\u0027s A \u0026 B specifier is totally fine and that sufficiency is a function of the context of the callback and the request, not the equality of a specifier. IE, apps using .equals() are already holding it wrong... since StringNetworkSpecifier was internal-only, it seemed that such a rough edge wasn\u0027t a big deal since \"don\u0027t do it.\"\n\n2) StringNetworkSpecifier is ambiguous (I mean, for telephony it\u0027s literally a number, not even a k/v pair), but I see that we added it to SystemApi for Christmas:\nhttps://android-review.googlesource.com/c/platform/frameworks/base/+/1198806\n\nIs it too late to not do that given that we are making TelephonyNetworkSpecifier? Anything we do to encourage or even support the continued use of StringNetworkSpecifier is an act of self-harm. I was really hoping to be done with StringNetworkSpecifier, in which case none of this is a problem and we can avoid any of the hacks you suggest (everybody wins).",
      "parentUuid": "4b209c0c_c6410311",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1319,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1320,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "cef2fccf376f97d07121e7aefddac23c8f116bfb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "153d2789_34b4e8a9",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/dataconnection/DataConnection.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 1320,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-06T13:07:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. No. The relation is not reflexive. Assuming the other elements of the request are satisfied, the request is satisfied if the request\u0027s specifier is satisfied by the network\u0027s specifier. At the limit, a null or MatchAllSpecifier satisfies all request specifiers.\n\n2. Sure, if we can avoid exposing StringNetworkSpecifier that would be preferable.\n\nBut I think neither of those address the point I was trying to make, do they? My point was that today, an app that does:\n\n    NetworkRequest r \u003d new NetworkRequest.Builder().setNetworkSpecifier(\"1\").build();\n\nwill match networks on the first SIM card. But after this CL, they will not.",
      "parentUuid": "6fca0645_1258bef7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1319,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1320,
        "endChar": 52
      },
      "revId": "cef2fccf376f97d07121e7aefddac23c8f116bfb",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
